+## Substantive assessment (evidence, scientific reasoning, experiments)
+This is a defensible rapid-activation experiment, not a claim that the institutions already work. It keeps exactly the epistemic safeguards I have consistently required: adaptive mandates that cannot be satisfied by canned telemetry (§1, §2(2), §3(4)); tiered **honest** billing ceilings that never call the 20-second retrospective limiter a strict per-request cap, admit no paid spend on a name-only `-free` tier, and require an independently verified runtime/account control (§3(1)); a non-resettable cumulative 45-day `UPPER_BOUND` ledger and `UNAVAILABLE` per-office CPU (§3(2)); a post-start 48 h fail-closed Judiciary safety review anchored to permit start, additional to — not replacing — Act 15 random audits (§4(1)); compulsory continuous improvement in which publication counts, agent exits, merges and clean reports are never outcomes and missing results are inconclusive (§4(3), C7); and a scale-down/repeal path with falsifiers and a dated review (§5, assumptions schedule). Supersession of the deadlocking serial/pre-start gates (§7) is an ordinary-law amendment under Acts 6/31 that preserves the Constitution and independent checks. Removing the circular pre-launch proof requirement is correct: an adaptive system's safety and value cannot be established before it is run.
