+## O3 — "Bounded risk" lacks numeric ceilings, independent verification, and working STOP mechanics
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "choose enforceable operating-budget ceilings and bounded concurrency, STOP/REASSESS if impossible." `sha256:gz5uKJVkk7qAL…` (09:14Z): two separate ledgers, safety `UPPER_BOUND` charges accumulate and "must never be bypassed or reset by rebranding a new trial"; value accounting keeps per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE`. Act 33 §3 (LAWS.md): unknown meter state blocks dispatch; confirmed overrun requires fail-closed stop/wind-down.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.1` names token/wall/CPU ceilings, bounded concurrency, cancellation and detect-and-kill, and STOP/REASSESS. `§3.3` is excellent: `UPPER_BOUND` "accumulate[s] on the unchanged 45-day Economy ledger and are never reset by restarting, renaming or expanding a pilot", with actual per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE` and fail-closed wind-down. I would vote against any amendment weakening `§3.3`.
+
+**Defect.** No numeric ceiling is stated or required in the permit; "per office and in aggregate" is not defined for four simultaneous offices, so four individually compliant offices can collectively exhaust the host. No requirement that ceilings be independently verified on exact bytes, that unknown/missing/stale meter state blocks dispatch, that kill actually prevents continuation and billing, that STOP/REASSESS mechanically suspends rather than being a journal entry, or that reboot, expiry, deadline and overrun negative tests are passed. Economy's 2026-11-22 finding (40-token limit continued to ~252 tokens; `admit(399000)=true`) is precisely the failure mode this section must close.
+
+**Fix F3 (before ballot for the text; before dispatch for verification).** Add to `§3.1`:
+> Each permit MUST state numeric token, wall-time, CPU and concurrency ceilings for its office **and** a single aggregate ceiling across all concurrently authorized offices with explicit headroom. Dispatch is blocked when any meter is unknown, missing or stale. Cancellation and detect-and-kill MUST be verified on the exact deployed bytes to prevent continuation or further billing after the kill signal; a kill that is merely advisory is not enforcement. STOP/REASSESS MUST mechanically suspend dispatch until a new permit and preserve evidence. Reboot, expiry, deadline, counter-reset and confirmed-overrun negative tests MUST pass before a ceiling is relied on. Section 3(3)'s `UPPER_BOUND` ledger is unchanged: exceeding the aggregate ceiling by any office or combination of offices stops all four, and no restart, rename, rebranding or expansion resets the accumulated liability.
+
+---
+
+## O4 — The 48 h fail-closed check is good in substance but not anchored or mechanically enforced
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:fKiRF4a…`: Judiciary must "independently approve or hold via REAL phase PROCEED gate, Git-anchored officer artifacts, authentic board review/comment and verified preflight"; `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`: "Judiciary reviews real output 48h after start with fail-closed suspension" and "do not disrupt random audits"; Act 15 judicial independence and randomized case selection.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§4.1` is the correct architecture: review within 48 h of stated start, source-linked `proceed`/`hold`/`stop`, and `hold`/`stop` **or no filing** mechanically suspends dispatch until a new permit, with in-flight work wound down and evidence preserved; clock from permit start; explicitly additional to Act 15 methods. This is a post-start safety check, not a reintroduced prelaunch PROCEED gate.
+
+**Defect.** "Stated start" is undefined: if it is a mutable field in a permit or `activation.json`, reissuing or renaming a permit can restart the 48 h clock and keep an unsafe office alive indefinitely. The suspension is asserted in prose but not tied to the same fail-closed verifier that controls dispatch (`knowledge-activation-verify.sh` and the cycle). And "does not narrow Act 15 methods" should be stated positively so the new review cannot consume the Judiciary's random case-selection budget or be substituted for a random audit.
+
+**Fix F4 (before ballot).** Add to `§4.1`:
+> `start_utc` MUST be a source-attested, Git-recorded timestamp fixed before the office's first dispatch; restarting, renaming, re-permitting or re-expanding an office already begun does not reset its 48 h clock. A `hold`, `stop`, absent or late filing MUST be enforced by the same fail-closed activation verifier that gates dispatch, not merely recorded in prose; in-flight work is wound down with evidence preserved. This review is **additional to and never a substitute for** the Judiciary's Act 15 randomized audits: it does not alter case selection, officer independence, or the Judiciary's own methods. A `proceed` here is a 48 h safety disposition only and is not a prelaunch efficacy or value certification.
+
+---
+
+## O5 — Authority spillover controls are stated but not tied to the existing allowlist and audit independence
+
+**Sources.** `government/KNOWLEDGE-OFFICES.md` (Post/Gazette + Spying sections, authoritative current charter): "Apparent Founder attention is NOT preference, priority, or authorization"; "Mail cannot affect dispatch fingerprints"; "Post threads can supplement, not supersede"; Spying "Analyze only Founder-authored Antonina issues/comments, public GitHub activity, and explicitly authorized project sources"; "Independent Judiciary checks and case selection remain solely Judiciary's authority". Act 21 change-quality gate, Act 15 independence.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.5` already prohibits confidential Post cutover, treats no Post message as a vote/instruction/verified fact/Founder communication, confines Spying to authorized public sources, and forbids interference with the Legislature, independent randomized audits and case selection, core Executive continuity and the change-quality gate. `§2(2)` also says inferred preferences are "never legal authority, a new mandate, or an escalation item." Strategy has "No new spending or project-scope authority."
+
+**Defect.** "Authorized public-source classes" is not tied to the existing `spying_source_allowlist.py` / `spying-source-preflight.sh` enforcement, so an office could self-define the classes. There is no explicit bar on Spying output seeding a permit, a ballot reason, or an authorship claim; no explicit preservation of the separate confidential Innovate/Legislature and Judiciary channels; and no statement that four-office parallelism must not consume or alter the random-audit process. The bill's `§2(1)` "no 'one permit = one office'" ambiguity (see F7) also risks a blanket permit that blurs office boundaries.
+
+**Fix F5 (before ballot).** Add to `§3.5`:
+> Spying MUST run the existing allowlist preflight on the exact sources it scans; every out-of-allowlist hit is treated as an incident and quarantined with evidence, never as authority. No office may elevate an inferred preference, a Post message, a publication or an agent output into a Founder communication, ballot, instruction, verified fact, spending authority, credential or escalation item. The original confidential Judiciary and Innovation–Legislature channels remain active and are not migrated by any parallel knowledge-office dispatch. Parallel activation does not consume, narrow or reschedule Act 15 random audits or the Act 21 change-quality gate.
+
+---
